;;; A rule's `:match` reads a name that starts with `neq` as a guard — M1e2
;;; **W22**, register row `W22/kw-neq-in-match` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`),
;;; banked by S1e2.2.22 (2026-11-05) from the inventory of 2026-11-05's F1, on
;;; the user's ruling of that day (Q-M1e2.16).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ──────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two facts, `(p a)` and `(neqw b)`, and one rule, `r`, concluding `(hit
;;; ?x)` from the premise `(neqw ?x)`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A keyword is a whole word: `00_ebnf.md` § 1, fact 1, excludes a reserved
;;; word from `SYMBOL` only where a word boundary follows it (`neq` is not a
;;; `SYMBOL`; `neq_test` is), so `neqw` is a name like any other. `r` fires on
;;; `(neqw b)`, so root holds `(hit b)`. The blind enumerator proposes `(hit
;;; a)`, `(neqw a)` and `(p b)`, and R is six states, the answer of
;;; `kw-neq-in-match-twin.ein`.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The parser reads the premise `(neqw ?x)` as the guard `(neq w ?x)`, which
;;; binds nothing, so every command that saturates stops at `r`, exit 1, with
;;; *unbound var ?x in :assert — bindings: {}* (`ein saturate`: prefixed
;;; `KeyError:`). (`ein solve FILE` at `35186bc0`, release and debug; every
;;; mode and `ein oracle` the same.)

(relation p Node) (relation neqw Node) (relation hit Node)
(p a) (neqw b)
(rule r () :match (neqw ?x) :assert (hit ?x))
